# Comments and class intent

Prefer **self-explanatory names and structure** over narrating obvious code. Comments should add what the code cannot say briefly.

## Language

- Write **all new or changed comments** (`//`, `/* */`, docblocks, and file-level technical notes in source) in **English**.
- **Do not** use other natural languages in comments you add or edit, even if nearby legacy comments differ.
- **Exceptions** (not “comments” in this sense): **string literals**, **user-visible messages**, **protocol or spec quotations**, and **identifiers** required by an external API or data contract—those follow product or interoperability needs.

## When to comment

- **Do** add comments for **non-obvious logic**, **subtle invariants**, **algorithm choices**, **performance or correctness tradeoffs**, and **edge cases** that are easy to misread.
- **Don’t** restate what the code already says (parameter names, trivial control flow, “what” without “why”).
- **Don’t** add verbose docblocks on every function unless the signature alone is insufficient.

## Class and struct documentation

- **Do** place a **short block** immediately before each `class` / `struct` definition (new or substantially edited) that states **core purpose** in one or two sentences: what it represents and what problem it solves.
- **Don’t** duplicate boilerplate that repeats the type name without adding meaning; keep it **intent-focused**.

Example:

```cpp
// Hosts the map viewport HWND and forwards paint to the Skia/RHI backend.
class MapViewportHost {
  // ...
};
```

## New or touched code

Apply these norms **in new or touched** translation units so documentation stays accurate without drive-by comment churn elsewhere.
